WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.215 --> 00:00:14.977
Mathilde was a pretty young woman married to Monsieur Loisel, a clerk in the education ministry.

2
00:00:16.398 --> 00:00:23.221
While he was content with their Paris apartment and ordinary, but comfortable, existence, Mathilde felt she deserved more.

3
00:00:24.902 --> 00:00:26.723
She longed for the finer things in life.

4
00:00:28.543 --> 00:00:35.015
Her desire was so great that she felt jealous every time she visited Jeanne Forestier, her wealthy friend.

5
00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:43.922
One January night Monsieur Loisel triumphantly presented Mathilde with an invitation to the education minister's ball.

6
00:00:45.343 --> 00:00:48.165
Mathilde frowned and tossed the invitation on the table.

7
00:00:49.266 --> 00:00:50.326
"I thought you'd be pleased!"

8
00:00:50.747 --> 00:00:51.227
he said.

9
00:00:51.247 --> 00:00:55.510
"Everyone wants to attend, and only a few clerks have been invited."

10
00:00:56.451 --> 00:00:57.712
"I have nothing to wear!"

11
00:00:59.028 --> 00:01:00.890
"What about the dress you wear to the theater?"

12
00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.673
"That's not appropriate for a ball!"

13
00:01:05.414 --> 00:01:08.056
Mathilde said as two tears rolled down her cheeks.

14
00:01:09.197 --> 00:01:12.940
"Give your invitation to a colleague whose wife can dress suitably!"

15
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.526
"My dear, how much would a dress cost—something you could wear on other occasions?"

16
00:01:20.426 --> 00:01:22.508
Mathilde calculated for a few seconds.

17
00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:29.281
She needed a sum high enough for a glamorous ball gown, but not so outrageous that her husband

18
00:01:29.301 --> 00:01:30.542
would instantly refuse.

19
00:01:30.562 --> 00:01:36.165
"I might manage with four hundred francs," she finally said.

20
00:01:36.965 --> 00:01:39.127
Monsieur Loisel turned pale.

21
00:01:40.467 --> 00:01:43.189
He'd saved that precise amount for a hunting trip.

22
00:01:44.429 --> 00:01:47.411
But, swallowing his disappointment, he gave her the money.

23
00:01:48.932 --> 00:01:52.694
As the ball drew near, Mathilde became more irritated and unhappy.

24
00:01:52.714 --> 00:01:57.337
"You have your lovely new dress," her husband said.

25
00:01:57.357 --> 00:01:59.859
"What could possibly be wrong?"

26
00:01:59.879 --> 00:02:03.462
"I don't own a single piece of jewelry."

27
00:02:03.482 --> 00:02:06.304
"You could wear some flowers."

28
00:02:06.324 --> 00:02:08.626
"And look poor?

29
00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:10.948
I'd rather not go at all!"

30
00:02:12.289 --> 00:02:14.071
Mathilde appeared close to tears.

31
00:02:14.091 --> 00:02:18.355
"I'm sure Jeanne would lend you something if you asked."

32
00:02:19.636 --> 00:02:21.377
Mathilde uttered a cry of joy.

33
00:02:21.397 --> 00:02:24.460
"Why didn't I think of that?"

34
00:02:26.816 --> 00:02:30.618
The next day Mathilde visited her friend, who was delighted to help.

35
00:02:31.619 --> 00:02:36.321
Mathilde tried on expensive bracelets and necklaces, but nothing seemed extravagant enough.

36
00:02:36.341 --> 00:02:39.383
"Don't you have anything else?"

37
00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:41.224
Mathilde asked impatiently.

38
00:02:41.244 --> 00:02:43.585
"Of course!"

39
00:02:44.726 --> 00:02:48.147
Jeanne brought out more boxes, which Mathilde eagerly opened.

40
00:02:49.248 --> 00:02:52.330
Finally Mathilde spotted an elegant diamond necklace.

41
00:02:53.450 --> 00:02:56.472
She swiftly scooped it up and fastened it around her neck.

42
00:02:57.532 --> 00:03:01.736
"This is all I need," she murmured as she gazed at her reflection.

43
00:03:03.537 --> 00:03:07.721
On the night of the ball, Mathilde was the prettiest woman at the minister's residence.

44
00:03:09.682 --> 00:03:12.625
Men wanted to dance with her, and women were jealous of her.

45
00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:20.712
By midnight her husband had fallen asleep in a chair, but she was still indulging in the attention

46
00:03:20.752 --> 00:03:21.853
she had long desired.

47
00:03:23.094 --> 00:03:28.898
At four o'clock in the morning, the ball ended, and Monsieur Loisel fetched Mathilde's cloak.

48
00:03:30.158 --> 00:03:32.900
"Wait here while I find a cab," he said at the door.

49
00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:40.024
But Mathilde was already running down the steps, fearful that the other women, dressed in expensive furs, would

50
00:03:40.084 --> 00:03:41.465
notice her everyday cloak.

51
00:03:42.985 --> 00:03:48.508
At home Mathilde wanted to see her beautiful reflection one last time, but what she saw in the

52
00:03:48.548 --> 00:03:50.009
mirror shocked her.

53
00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.752
"I've—I've—I've lost Jeanne's necklace!"

54
00:03:55.656 --> 00:03:58.078
"Impossible! It must be here someplace."

55
00:03:59.379 --> 00:04:02.902
Together the Loisels examined her dress and cloak but found nothing.

56
00:04:02.922 --> 00:04:08.386
"If you had lost it in the street, we would have heard it fall," Monsieur Loisel said.

57
00:04:08.406 --> 00:04:10.367
"It must be in the cab!"

58
00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:14.230
He flew out of the house to retrace their steps.

59
00:04:15.071 --> 00:04:20.695
He visited the cab company, the police, and finally the newspapers, where he placed an ad offering a

60
00:04:20.755 --> 00:04:29.142
reward. Hours later he returned home empty-handed and found Mathilde waiting, still in her ball dress and too

61
00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:30.163
distraught to move.

62
00:04:31.403 --> 00:04:37.186
"Write to Jeanne and say you've broken the necklace's clasp and are having it repaired," he said urgently.

63
00:04:37.206 --> 00:04:38.666
"I have a plan."

64
00:04:40.587 --> 00:04:44.649
That week they went from jeweler to jeweler, searching for a similar necklace.

65
00:04:45.509 --> 00:04:48.970
At last they found one, but it cost forty thousand francs!

66
00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:54.208
"I'll sell it for 36,000," the jeweler offered.

67
00:04:54.228 --> 00:04:59.110
"If you find your necklace within a month, I'll buy back this one for 34,000."

68
00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:01.890
They knew what they must do.

69
00:05:03.611 --> 00:05:09.252
Monsieur Loisel had inherited 18,000 francs; he borrowed the rest from friends and lenders.

70
00:05:10.993 --> 00:05:15.794
Mathilde returned the new necklace to Jeanne, hoping she wouldn't examine it too carefully.

71
00:05:17.175 --> 00:05:20.175
After that, the Loisels' lives changed dramatically.

72
00:05:20.736 --> 00:05:24.926
To pay the horrible debt, they dismissed their servant and moved to an attic room.

73
00:05:26.107 --> 00:05:29.409
Mathilde endured the housework and any other work she could find.

74
00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:34.733
It took ten years for the Loisels to pay back all their loans plus interest.

75
00:05:35.433 --> 00:05:37.775
Mathilde now appeared old and exhausted.

76
00:05:39.676 --> 00:05:44.760
One day while walking in the park, she encountered Jeanne who still looked youthful and beautiful.

77
00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:49.323
"Jeanne!" "Do I know you?"

78
00:05:49.763 --> 00:05:52.865
Jeanne asked, surprised that this stranger was addressing her.

79
00:05:54.446 --> 00:05:56.246
"It's me—Mathilde!"

80
00:05:56.266 --> 00:06:00.828
"Oh, my poor Mathilde, how you've changed!"

81
00:06:00.848 --> 00:06:05.750
"Yes, and it's all your fault for lending me that necklace."

82
00:06:06.691 --> 00:06:09.992
Mathilde recounted the story of her descent into poverty.

83
00:06:10.012 --> 00:06:14.374
"I never realized that the necklace wasn't the original!"

84
00:06:15.554 --> 00:06:18.916
Tears filled Jeanne's eyes as she grasped Mathilde's hands.

85
00:06:20.072 --> 00:06:22.957
"But, Mathilde, those diamonds weren't real.

86
00:06:23.939 --> 00:06:26.603
My necklace was worth only five hundred francs!"
